1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,455
You bought your first crypto.

2
00:00:01,455 --> 00:00:03,442
You can see the balance on the screen.

3
00:00:03,442 --> 00:00:04,687
So here is the question

4
00:00:04,687 --> 00:00:06,655
almost nobody asks about it.

5
00:00:06,655 --> 00:00:08,166
Who is actually holding it?

6
00:00:08,166 --> 00:00:09,397
Because on most platforms,

7
00:00:09,397 --> 00:00:11,116
the answer is not you.

8
00:00:11,116 --> 00:00:12,239
Stay to the end

9
00:00:12,239 --> 00:00:13,847
and you will know which of the two kinds

10
00:00:13,847 --> 00:00:14,459
you are using,

11
00:00:14,459 --> 00:00:15,747
and the one thing to do

12
00:00:15,747 --> 00:00:17,673
the moment a trade settles.

13
00:00:17,673 --> 00:00:18,733
Start with the number

14
00:00:18,733 --> 00:00:20,536
that surprises people most.

15
00:00:20,536 --> 00:00:21,425
Zero.

16
00:00:21,425 --> 00:00:23,664
That is how many private keys you hold

17
00:00:23,664 --> 00:00:24,563
when your coins sit

18
00:00:24,563 --> 00:00:26,445
on a centralized exchange.

19
00:00:26,445 --> 00:00:28,501
The company holds every one of them.

20
00:00:28,501 --> 00:00:31,239
So what is an exchange, actually?

21
00:00:31,239 --> 00:00:33,189
An exchange is a marketplace.

22
00:00:33,189 --> 00:00:35,000
Nothing more mysterious than that.

23
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,969
It matches somebody who wants to buy

24
00:00:36,969 --> 00:00:38,795
with somebody who wants to sell,

25
00:00:38,795 --> 00:00:39,918
and takes a small cut

26
00:00:39,918 --> 00:00:41,322
for standing in the middle.

27
00:00:41,322 --> 00:00:43,243
It is also where the price comes from,

28
00:00:43,243 --> 00:00:44,761
and where liquidity lives.

29
00:00:44,761 --> 00:00:45,990
Liquidity just means

30
00:00:45,990 --> 00:00:47,757
there are enough buyers and sellers

31
00:00:47,757 --> 00:00:48,954
that your order fills

32
00:00:48,954 --> 00:00:51,252
at roughly the price you expected.

33
00:00:51,252 --> 00:00:52,615
But how that middle is built

34
00:00:52,615 --> 00:00:54,412
is where the two families split.

35
00:00:54,412 --> 00:00:55,829
One of them is a company.

36
00:00:55,829 --> 00:00:57,575
The other is a piece of code.

37
00:00:57,575 --> 00:00:58,792
And that single difference

38
00:00:58,792 --> 00:01:00,806
decides everything else about them.

39
00:01:00,806 --> 00:01:03,683
A company, or a smart contract?

40
00:01:03,683 --> 00:01:05,994
A centralized exchange is a business.

41
00:01:05,994 --> 00:01:08,795
Coinbase. Binance. Crypto.com.

42
00:01:08,795 --> 00:01:10,157
It runs an order book,

43
00:01:10,157 --> 00:01:11,371
it checks who you are,

44
00:01:11,371 --> 00:01:12,318
and it keeps your coins

45
00:01:12,318 --> 00:01:14,074
in wallets that it controls.

46
00:01:14,074 --> 00:01:15,732
A decentralized exchange

47
00:01:15,732 --> 00:01:17,455
has no company in it at all.

48
00:01:17,455 --> 00:01:18,842
You connect your own wallet,

49
00:01:18,842 --> 00:01:20,824
a smart contract does the swap,

50
00:01:20,824 --> 00:01:22,876
and your keys never leave your hands.

51
00:01:22,876 --> 00:01:25,430
Most of them do not even run an order book.

52
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:27,005
You trade against a pool of coins

53
00:01:27,005 --> 00:01:28,384
that other users put there,

54
00:01:28,384 --> 00:01:30,432
and those users take the fees.

55
00:01:30,432 --> 00:01:31,559
So look at what each one

56
00:01:31,559 --> 00:01:32,856
actually hands you.

57
00:01:32,856 --> 00:01:34,841
Deep liquidity and fast fills.

58
00:01:34,841 --> 00:01:36,577
Cash in, and cash out.

59
00:01:36,577 --> 00:01:38,004
The company holds your keys.

60
00:01:38,004 --> 00:01:39,026
You keep your keys.

61
00:01:39,026 --> 00:01:40,421
No identity check.

62
00:01:40,421 --> 00:01:42,437
Thinner markets, and network fees.

63
00:01:42,437 --> 00:01:44,208
Neither column is the winner.

64
00:01:44,208 --> 00:01:44,930
They are answers

65
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:46,562
to two different questions.

66
00:01:46,562 --> 00:01:48,679
What is a trading pair?

67
00:01:48,679 --> 00:01:50,514
One thing confuses every beginner,

68
00:01:50,514 --> 00:01:52,678
and it is written on every screen.

69
00:01:52,678 --> 00:01:55,267
You will see it written as BTC / USD.

70
00:01:55,267 --> 00:01:57,161
The first one is what you are buying.

71
00:01:57,161 --> 00:01:59,204
The second is what you are paying with.

72
00:01:59,204 --> 00:02:00,893
That is the whole idea.

73
00:02:00,893 --> 00:02:02,370
A price only means something

74
00:02:02,370 --> 00:02:04,069
measured against something else.

75
00:02:04,069 --> 00:02:05,807
And they come in three kinds.

76
00:02:05,807 --> 00:02:07,395
Crypto against real money.

77
00:02:07,395 --> 00:02:09,398
Crypto against other crypto.

78
00:02:09,398 --> 00:02:11,517
Crypto against a stablecoin.

79
00:02:11,517 --> 00:02:13,195
Which pairs a platform offers

80
00:02:13,195 --> 00:02:15,001
decides what you can buy directly,

81
00:02:15,001 --> 00:02:16,871
and what costs you three trades

82
00:02:16,871 --> 00:02:18,357
and three sets of fees.

83
00:02:18,357 --> 00:02:21,066
Not your keys, not your coins

84
00:02:21,066 --> 00:02:23,269
Now the part that catches people out.

85
00:02:23,269 --> 00:02:24,549
The friendlier platform

86
00:02:24,549 --> 00:02:26,372
is the one where you own nothing.

87
00:02:26,372 --> 00:02:28,816
A centralized exchange feels safe.

88
00:02:28,816 --> 00:02:30,116
There is a support line.

89
00:02:30,116 --> 00:02:31,479
There is a password reset.

90
00:02:31,479 --> 00:02:33,443
It looks like online banking.

91
00:02:33,443 --> 00:02:35,198
But those are exactly the features

92
00:02:35,198 --> 00:02:36,660
that require somebody else

93
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:38,100
to be holding the keys.

94
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:39,297
Your balance is a row

95
00:02:39,297 --> 00:02:41,122
in that company's database.

96
00:02:41,122 --> 00:02:43,298
The coins behind it sit in their wallets,

97
00:02:43,298 --> 00:02:45,357
pooled with everybody else's.

98
00:02:45,357 --> 00:02:47,083
There is an old line about this

99
00:02:47,083 --> 00:02:48,979
and it has outlasted every exchange

100
00:02:48,979 --> 00:02:50,031
that ignored it.

101
00:02:50,031 --> 00:02:54,040
Not your keys, not your coins.

102
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:55,419
So do not treat an exchange

103
00:02:55,419 --> 00:02:56,633
like a bank account.

104
00:02:56,633 --> 00:02:58,341
It is a place you pass through.

105
00:02:58,341 --> 00:03:00,631
So how do you choose one?

106
00:03:00,631 --> 00:03:01,946
Before you trust any platform

107
00:03:01,946 --> 00:03:03,165
with a single coin,

108
00:03:03,165 --> 00:03:04,931
run the same five checks.

109
00:03:04,931 --> 00:03:07,345
Two-factor authentication from an app.

110
00:03:07,345 --> 00:03:10,429
Most coins held offline, in cold storage.

111
00:03:10,429 --> 00:03:12,369
A licence in a country you have heard of.

112
00:03:12,369 --> 00:03:14,692
Fees you can find without hunting.

113
00:03:14,692 --> 00:03:18,328
A history you can look up.

114
00:03:18,328 --> 00:03:19,115
Miss one of those

115
00:03:19,115 --> 00:03:21,047
and you are not choosing an exchange.

116
00:03:21,047 --> 00:03:22,222
You are hoping.

117
00:03:22,222 --> 00:03:23,289
And once you are in,

118
00:03:23,289 --> 00:03:25,152
four habits do most of the work.

119
00:03:25,152 --> 00:03:27,049
Turn the two-factor on first.

120
00:03:27,049 --> 00:03:29,395
Start with an amount you can afford to lose.

121
00:03:29,395 --> 00:03:32,038
Learn limit orders before market orders.

122
00:03:32,038 --> 00:03:33,515
Then move what you are not trading

123
00:03:33,515 --> 00:03:34,788
off the platform.

124
00:03:34,788 --> 00:03:36,874
A limit order waits for your price.

125
00:03:36,874 --> 00:03:39,178
A market order takes whatever is there.

126
00:03:39,178 --> 00:03:40,743
That difference costs beginners

127
00:03:40,743 --> 00:03:42,046
more than the fees do.

128
00:03:42,046 --> 00:03:44,232
So — back to the balance on your screen.

129
00:03:44,232 --> 00:03:45,316
The question was never

130
00:03:45,316 --> 00:03:47,192
which exchange is the best one.

131
00:03:47,192 --> 00:03:51,543
It is: who is holding it right now?

132
00:03:51,543 --> 00:03:52,239
Know that,

133
00:03:52,239 --> 00:03:53,056
and you already know

134
00:03:53,056 --> 00:03:54,771
whether it should still be sitting there.

135
00:03:54,771 --> 00:03:56,919
The full comparison and the platform reviews

136
00:03:56,919 --> 00:03:58,227
are linked below —

137
00:03:58,227 --> 00:04:00,662
and nothing here is financial advice.

138
00:04:00,662 --> 00:04:01,772
If that was useful,

139
00:04:01,772 --> 00:04:04,723
subscribe for more videos like this one.
